{"step_index":7030,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T14:16:10Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-25T14:16:10Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-25T14:16:14Z\nThe search for \"\"iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation\" override organization policy google cloud console steps\" returned the following summary:\nTo override the `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation` organization policy, you must have the **Organization Policy Administrator** role (`roles/orgpolicy.policyAdmin`) assigned at the **organization level**[1][2][3].\n\n### Important Prerequisites\n*   **Permissions:** You must have the `roles/orgpolicy.policyAdmin` role[1][4]. If you do not see this option, ensure you are selecting your **Organization** in the Google Cloud Console project picker, not a specific project[5][3].\n*   **Hierarchy:** If the policy is enforced at the organization level, you can typically override it for a specific project by creating an exception[1][6]. However, if the policy is configured to prevent overrides or is enforced strictly, you may need to consult your organization's administrator[7][8].\n\n### Steps to Override the Policy\n1.  **Navigate to Organization Policies:**\n    *   Go to the [Organization Policies](https://console.cloud.google.com/iam-admin/orgpolicies) page in the Google Cloud Console[1][9].\n    *   In the resource selector at the top-left, **select your Organization** (the top-most node in your hierarchy)[1][3].\n2.  **Find the Constraint:**\n    *   In the filter bar, search for `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation`[10][1].\n    *   Click on the policy name to open its details page[1].\n3.  **Override the Policy for a Project:**\n    *   Click **Edit**[1][2].\n    *   Under **Policy source**, select **Override parent's policy**[11][1].\n    *   Change the enforcement setting from **Enforced** to **Not enforced** (or customize it to exclude specific projects if supported)[1][6].\n    *   Click **Save**[1][12].\n\n### If the Override Option is Grayed Out\nIf you have the correct roles but still cannot override the policy, it is likely because:\n